  "item": "mod[tests]/impl[Project/Domain]/fn[node_path]",
  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Source-tool tests publish OutlineId addresses and retain mapped NodeIds\n directly in dependencies. This required Domain method is unused in\n those flows; stable-path and filesystem-storage tests use other domains."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/project_sources.rs` `mod[tests]/impl[Project/Domain]/fn[node_path]` is a valid function-level coverage exception.\n\n- **Form:** The citation is a fenced `:::{cite} habeas:coverage` block directly on an explicit function with a body, and the commentary supplies the explanation.\n- **Reason is substantive:** The commentary says the method is a required `Domain` method that the source-tool tests never reach. It is not just calling the code hard to cover.\n- **Source-tool tests do not call it:**\n  - The tests in `project_sources.rs` publish `OutlineId` addresses and map them through `SourceNodes::source_node`.\n  - They assert `Dependency::Node(\"source-node\".into(), fingerprint)` directly.\n  - They use `Project`'s own `load_evidence` and `store_evidence`. Those ignore the node and do not call `node_path`.\n- **Production callers are not reached:**\n  - The callers of `node_path` are the agent graph tools in `agent/tools.rs` (`ToolAgent::run`, `GraphSession::node`, `GraphSession::dispatch`) and `edge_fingerprint` in `graph.rs`.\n  - `Project` has no graph edges, since its `outgoing_edges` is empty, and the source-tool tests do not use the graph tools.\n  - `edge_fingerprint` is only used for `OutgoingEdges` dependencies.\n- **Other domains cover stable paths and storage:** `tests/contracts.rs`, `tests/validation.rs` and `tests/storage.rs` exercise `node_path` and filesystem-storage paths with their own domains. This matches the commentary's last claim.\n- **No redundancy:** The item has no {impl} or {test} reference to the cited built-in, so there is no redundant citation.",
